3. Farm Overview / Daily Tracker
3.1 Main question
“What is happening on this farm today, which field needs attention, and what is the next useful action?”
3.2 Content order
1. Farm identity, location, mapped area, field count, active assessment status, evidence freshness.
2. Selected-field control, visually prominent.
3. Current conditions: temperature, high/low, wind, humidity, latest precipitation and timestamp; forecast only when forecast data exists.
4. Current crop: crop/variety, actual planting date, stage (observed or estimated), season/cycle position and expected maturity window where supportable.
5. Water status: estimated ETc/demand, observed rain, modelled effective rain, confirmed irrigation, root-zone depletion/status and next irrigation review where valid.
6. Soil summary: only available key soil properties and source type.
7. Field map preview positioned in the lower-left area of the overview, showing actual saved geometry on a real map. It is a preview, not a decorative map image. Clicking it opens Maps with the same farm, field and layer/date context.
8. Active alerts, highest severity first, each linked to evidence and field.
9. One or more evidence-backed next actions with due windows.
10. Meaningful changes since last comparable report.
11. Field table: field name, mapped area, crop, stage, condition, alerts, analysis freshness.
Do not add unrelated portfolio analytics, decorative activity panels or duplicated metrics just to fill empty space. The overview is a scan surface; full tables and equations belong to their specialist pages.
3.3 Farm-wide roll-up
- Show number of fields assessed / total fields.
- Show count/distribution by status.
- Show fields with active critical/high issues separately.
- Aggregate water volumes only from field results on the same time basis and compatible units.
- Do not allow a healthy large field to hide a critical small field.
- Do not average unlike indicators (e.g. NDVI, soil pH, mm water deficit, risk severity, confidence) into an undocumented one-number “health score”.
3.4 “What changed?” logic
Show only meaningful, comparable changes. Each entry identifies whether it is a new observation, new farmer input, recalculation, revised geometry, source/product change or model-version change. Do not count a mere data retrieval timestamp change as an agronomic improvement or deterioration.
