PART B — DATA ACQUISITION AND SOURCE INTEGRATIONS
5. Farm/field location and geometry
5.1 Inputs
- User-entered farm address or place.
- Explicit latitude/longitude, optionally geocoded.
- Optional browser geolocation, only after permission.
- Independently drawn/imported farm and field polygons.
- Field geometry version, CRS, source/import method, area, perimeter, validity results and effective date.
A location pin and field polygon are different entities. A farm may contain many fields; do not save the whole farm as one undifferentiated field polygon.
5.2 Map and geometry logic
- Use a real map provider and retain attribution/terms.
- Support draw, edit vertices, name, delete with confirmation, undo/redo, save/cancel, import and validation.
- Compute area geodesically or in a suitable equal-area projected CRS; never calculate hectares from raw degrees.
- Validate self-intersections, duplicate vertices, invalid rings, overlap, implausible size and CRS mismatch. Warn and let the farmer review; do not silently “correct” a boundary.
- Polygon edits create a new GeometryVersion; previous reports keep the old version.
- Field geometry drives raster intersections, weather sampling, mapped area and data-coverage calculations.
5.3 Spatial sampling rule
Do not assume a point weather API or coarse raster is a true measurement for the whole field. Record source cell size, pixel count, polygon overlap and sample method. Where the provider supports polygon/raster extraction, sample valid intersecting cells and compute a documented area-weighted statistic. Where it only accepts a coordinate, use an explicitly labelled representative point/centroid and disclose the point/grid limitation. For a small field smaller than the source grid, label the output as grid-scale or regional context, not field-specific measurement.
6. Weather and climate acquisition
6.1 Coordinate selection
For each field, query its polygon/centroid and source grid. Store the exact query coordinates or geometry hash, elevation if needed, timezone, returned grid/cell, model and valid time. When the field is large or spans multiple weather cells, use spatial sampling where the provider permits; otherwise disclose a single-point approximation.
6.2 Forecast API adapter
A forecast provider such as Open-Meteo can supply current, hourly and daily model values. Candidate variables include:
- Current/hourly: temperature_2m, relative_humidity_2m, dew_point_2m, precipitation, rain, wind_speed_10m, wind_direction_10m, wind_gusts_10m, shortwave_radiation, vapour_pressure_deficit, et0_fao_evapotranspiration where offered by the selected endpoint/model.
- Daily: temperature_2m_max, temperature_2m_min, precipitation_sum, precipitation_probability_max, wind_speed_10m_max, wind_gusts_10m_max, wind_direction_10m_dominant, shortwave_radiation_sum, et0_fao_evapotranspiration where supported.
Example adapter request (verify variable availability and commercial-use terms before production):
https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast
  ?latitude={lat}
  &longitude={lon}
  &current=temperature_2m,relative_humidity_2m,precipitation,wind_speed_10m,wind_direction_10m,wind_gusts_10m
  &hourly=temperature_2m,relative_humidity_2m,dew_point_2m,precipitation,rain,wind_speed_10m,wind_direction_10m,wind_gusts_10m,shortwave_radiation,vapour_pressure_deficit,et0_fao_evapotranspiration
  &daily=temperature_2m_max,temperature_2m_min,precipitation_sum,precipitation_probability_max,wind_speed_10m_max,wind_gusts_10m_max,wind_direction_10m_dominant,shortwave_radiation_sum,et0_fao_evapotranspiration
  &timezone=auto
The frontend does not call the provider directly. The backend adapter validates the response, stores provider/model metadata, normalizes units, and returns Field Shift DTOs. Forecast data is always labelled Forecast, with issue/retrieval time and valid time.
6.3 Historical weather and climate
Use a historical/reanalysis API (e.g. Open-Meteo Historical Weather, NASA POWER Daily) for daily/hourly historic variables and climate baselines, after choosing a dataset with appropriate spatial and temporal resolution. Reanalysis fills gaps using a model; it is not the same as a local weather-station observation. Keep datasets consistent within long-term comparisons; do not silently splice different models or versions.

NASA POWER Daily is an analysis-ready daily solar/meteorological time series, offers daily average/max/min parameters, and has explicit time-standard behaviour (the daily API defaults to local solar time unless requested otherwise). Set and persist the intended time standard, preferably UTC for internal joins with explicit conversion to farm timezone for presentation. Check original source resolution for every parameter.

Example NASA POWER adapter request (verify parameter IDs, community, unit and returned metadata against the live API before use):
https://power.larc.nasa.gov/api/temporal/daily/point
  ?parameters=T2M,T2M_MAX,T2M_MIN,PRECTOTCORR,WS2M,RH2M,ALLSKY_SFC_SW_DWN
  &community=AG
  &longitude={lon}
  &latitude={lat}
  &start={YYYYMMDD}
  &end={YYYYMMDD}
  &format=JSON
  &time-standard=UTC
6.4 Weather values shown
- Current temperature and apparent temperature, if supplied.
- Daily minimum / maximum / mean temperature.
- Relative humidity, dew point and VPD when inputs are valid.
- Wind speed/direction/gusts and measurement height.
- Rainfall / precipitation amount and hours.
- Forecast rain probability and predicted amount, separately from actual rainfall.
- Solar radiation / sunshine.
- Pressure where relevant.
- Reference ET₀ with method and source.
- Heat/cold/frost exposure only against verified crop-specific thresholds and stages.
- Dry-spell length and rainfall anomaly against a comparable baseline.
- GDD accumulation and threshold progress.
6.5 Derived climate metrics
Daily/period rainfall: sum observations over a precise timezone-aware period; do not sum forecast into observed history.

Rainfall anomaly:

anomaly_mm = observed_period_rain_mm - baseline_period_mean_mm

Also show anomaly percent only when baseline is nonzero and the periods/season definitions match. Store baseline years, dataset, period and method.

Growing degree days:

GDD_day = max(0, (Tmax_capped + Tmin_floored) / 2 - Tbase)

The crop record defines base/upper temperatures, caps/floors and accumulation rule. Sum from a known phenological start date. Record any missing-day fill policy; do not silently fill gaps.

VPD: derive saturation vapour pressure from temperature and actual vapour pressure from humidity/dew point using a cited, unit-checked method; VPD = es - ea. Do not calculate if the input semantics/units are incompatible.

Dry spell: count consecutive days meeting a documented rainfall threshold; threshold is metric-versioned and location/crop use is disclosed.

Heat exposure: compare valid hourly/daily temperatures to crop-specific thresholds for the active crop stage and sensitive period. Store degree-hours/days above threshold only where the model defines them. Do not claim crop damage from exposure alone.
6.6 Weather API availability states
Every module has Connected, Stale, Partial, Unavailable or Planned status derived from actual requests. A forecast API failure must not replace the latest successful historical data with zeros or fake forecasts. Record provider, model, query point/grid, unit, period, retrieval time and error class.
7. Crop knowledge and growth-stage acquisition
7.1 Critical correction: crop stages are not simply fetched from a universal weather API
Weather APIs provide weather data. They do not generally know the exact variety, sowing/transplant date, management practice, local stage duration, phenological observations or cultivar response of the user's crop. There is no safe universal endpoint that returns the exact number of days each stage will take for every cultivar and field.

Build a versioned CropKnowledge library backed by reviewed sources (e.g. FAO Irrigation and Drainage Paper 56 crop parameters; crop-specific research/extension data; locally validated variety parameters; official crop calendars where applicable). Where a suitable crop model or crop-data API is integrated, treat it as another sourced parameter provider, not an oracle. Each parameter must retain crop species, cultivar/variety, climate/region applicability, units, source URL/reference, publication/version, uncertainty and validation status.
7.2 Required crop record
- Crop ID; common/scientific name; crop family; variety/cultivar.
- Annual/perennial behaviour; production purpose (grain, forage, vegetable, fruit, etc.).
- Planting method: direct seed / transplant / perennial establishment / other.
- Typical planting windows by local agro-climatic zone, if sourced.
- Minimum/optimum/maximum temperature parameters and relevant stage sensitivities.
- GDD base and cap/floor values, when a validated thermal-time model exists.
- Total duration bounds and stage durations or stage GDD thresholds.
- Stage definitions and stage-transition rules.
- Kc/Kcb values for initial, development, mid-season and late-season stages; source and climate adjustments.
- Rooting depth progression by crop stage, not just a single maximum.
- Root-zone depletion fraction p / MAD guidance where supported.
- Crop water sensitivity during critical stages.
- Nutrient uptake curves and crop removal coefficients only where sourced.
- Crop-specific pH, salinity, drainage and texture tolerances where evidenced.
- Crop family/host constraints relevant to rotation.
- Evidence quality and version.
Unknown parameters remain unknown. Do not create a complete profile merely from a crop name.
7.3 Crop occurrence record (per field)
- Field ID and crop occurrence ID.
- Crop ID and cultivar ID.
- Actual planting/sowing/transplant date, date certainty and source.
- Planned date/window separately from actual date.
- Emergence date, flowering/heading/fruit set dates, maturity/harvest date if observed.
- Seed rate/plant density if recorded.
- Irrigation and nutrient program references.
- Current stage: farmer-observed / sensor-observed / model-estimated.
- Historical crop sequence and prior crop residue/legume information.
Never infer that the recommended rotation is already planted. A plan and an actual crop occurrence are different records.
7.4 Stage estimation pipeline
1. Read the field's current crop occurrence and exact planting/transplant date.
2. Read crop species, cultivar and production system.
3. Load the crop's versioned stage definitions, thermal thresholds and calendar durations.
4. Fetch daily temperatures for the period since planting from the selected trusted dataset.
5. Apply crop-specific GDD caps/floors and accumulate GDD.
6. Compare accumulated GDD with local/cultivar stage transition thresholds, when those exist.
7. Compare with calendar-duration estimate as a consistency check, not an independent fact.
8. Incorporate farmer-observed stage/date. A confirmed observation takes precedence for actual stage while modelled stage is retained for comparison.
9. Estimate flowering, maturity and harvest windows only if parameters and data support it. Propagate uncertainty.
10. Save the estimated stage with input snapshot, calculation version, confidence and next review trigger.
If only calendar stage durations exist, use those as a lower-confidence estimate. If actual planting date is missing, display Stage unavailable — confirm planting date; do not calculate precise days remaining.
7.5 Stage labels
The four FAO-56 crop-coefficient stages are Initial, Crop development, Mid-season, Late season. They are water-coefficient phases, not a complete universal botanic stage vocabulary. Add crop-specific phenology labels (e.g. emergence, vegetative, flowering, grain filling, maturity) from crop-specific sources. Map detailed phenology stages to Kc/Kcb phases using an explicit crop mapping table.
PART C — WATER AND IRRIGATION ENGINE (DETAILED)
8. Water Intelligence: inputs required before computing recommendations
The backend must assemble all of the following for each field and date/period:
8.1 Weather inputs
- Daily/hourly Tmax, Tmin, Tmean.
- Relative humidity/dew point or actual vapour pressure.
- Wind speed converted to the required 2 m reference height (u2) using a documented height adjustment when needed.
- Solar/net radiation inputs.
- Atmospheric pressure/elevation if required.
- Observed rainfall, with source/product/time.
- Forecast rainfall in a separate forecast dataset.
- ET₀ and its calculation/provider method.
8.2 Crop inputs
- Crop/cultivar and crop purpose.
- Actual planting date and current stage.
- Stage-specific Kc or Kcb values and their source.
- Stage duration or thermal thresholds.
- Rooting depth at current stage.
- Crop depletion fraction p / MAD parameter.
- Critical sensitivity windows and stress thresholds.
- Crop coefficient adjustments for local humidity, wind, crop height and wetting pattern where the FAO method calls for them.
8.3 Soil and water inputs
- Field capacity theta_FC and wilting point theta_WP for relevant layers/depths, with source and uncertainty.
- Soil texture/profile/depth and restrictive layers.
- Available water capacity where measured or suitably estimated.
- Current soil moisture/depletion from calibrated in-field sensor if available; sensor location/depth/calibration must be stored.
- Otherwise, a modelled initial root-zone depletion with an explicit source/assumption and uncertainty.
- Effective root depth by crop stage.
- Infiltration/hydraulic conductivity/runoff parameters when the model includes them.
- Drainage and water-table/capillary-rise context when supported.
- Irrigation system type, method and measured/declared application efficiency.
- Irrigation source, reliable supply/capacity and any seasonal cap.
- Actual irrigation events; keep routine separate.
8.4 Management and field operations
- Irrigation date/time and actual applied depth/volume.
- Irrigation event area and affected zone.
- Method, pump/sector flow, emitter/nozzle spacing, application rate and pressure/flow evidence where runtime is calculated.
- Irrigation routine frequency and quantity, each independently confirmed and freshness-dated.
- Mulch/cover/residue, tillage or other practice only when entered/confirmed and when the selected model accounts for it.
If required inputs are missing, calculate only the outputs that remain scientifically supportable. Example: ET₀/ETc can be estimated without a soil profile, but exact field irrigation timing/root-zone depletion cannot.
9. Reference evapotranspiration ET₀
9.1 Primary method
Use FAO-56 Penman–Monteith when all required meteorological inputs are available and validated. Daily form:
ET0 = [0.408 * Delta * (Rn - G)
       + gamma * (900 / (T + 273)) * u2 * (es - ea)]
      / [Delta + gamma * (1 + 0.34 * u2)]
Where:
- ET0: reference evapotranspiration, mm/day.
- Rn: net radiation at crop surface, MJ/m²/day.
- G: soil heat flux, MJ/m²/day (daily approximation may be zero only where method permits and is stated).
- T: mean daily air temperature at 2 m, °C.
- u2: wind speed at 2 m, m/s.
- es: saturation vapour pressure, kPa.
- ea: actual vapour pressure, kPa.
- Delta: slope of saturation vapour pressure curve, kPa/°C.
- gamma: psychrometric constant, kPa/°C.
Implement every intermediate calculation with unit conversions and validation. Record method version, input variables, time standard and missing-data policy.
9.2 ET₀ provider alternative
If a provider returns a documented FAO reference ET₀ variable (for example Open-Meteo's et0_fao_evapotranspiration), the service may use it as provider-supplied ET₀ after checking model, definition, units, time-step, licence and missing values. Store provider provenance and do not claim Field Shift independently ran Penman–Monteith unless it did.
9.3 Incomplete weather data
If wind/radiation/humidity is missing, do not label a temperature-only or incomplete-data proxy FAO-56. Either use a scientifically documented alternative method with explicit name/limitations or show ET₀ unavailable. Do not quietly invent missing weather inputs.
10. Crop evapotranspiration ETc and crop stage
10.1 Single crop coefficient method
For standard, well-watered crop conditions:

ETc = Kc * ET0

Kc is selected/interpolated from the field crop occurrence's stage-specific curve. It must not be a single generic coefficient for the whole crop season.

The service:
1. Determines stage from planting date + phenology/GDD + observed stage.
2. Loads Kc_ini, Kc_mid, Kc_end and stage durations from the versioned crop knowledge base.
3. Builds an interpolation curve through initial → development → mid-season → late-season.
4. Adjusts coefficients for relevant climate, wind, humidity, crop height and wetting frequency using the documented method where required.
5. Multiplies the correct period's ET₀ by Kc.
6. Stores the exact curve point, ET₀ and Kc used.
10.2 Dual crop coefficient for daily irrigation management
Where the input data supports daily soil evaporation, use:

ETc = (Kcb + Ke) * ET0

Under water stress:

ETc_adj = (Ks * Kcb + Ke) * ET0

Where Kcb represents basal crop transpiration, Ke represents soil evaporation, and Ks reduces transpiration under root-zone stress. This is generally more physically informative for daily scheduling than applying a stress coefficient indiscriminately to soil evaporation.

Ke requires soil-surface wetting/depletion, exposed/wetted soil fraction, rainfall/irrigation events and evaporation-layer data. If these are absent, use the supported single-Kc method and disclose its limitation.
10.3 Crop water demand display
Show:
- ET₀ (mm/day) and method/source.
- Crop and crop occurrence.
- Current stage and how determined.
- Kc/Kcb and source/version.
- Ke and Ks when calculated.
- Potential ETc (no water stress) and adjusted ETc (stress adjusted) separately.
- Daily demand and cumulative demand for selected period.
- Field volume equivalent: m³ = ETc_mm * area_ha * 10.
- Confidence and missing inputs.
ETc is crop evapotranspiration, not automatically the exact amount the irrigation system should apply that day. Irrigation scheduling must also consider stored soil water, effective rainfall, application efficiency and root-zone limits.
11. Rainfall, effective precipitation and source reconciliation
11.1 Store separate rainfall concepts
- Observed station rainfall.
- Gridded/reanalysis rainfall.
- Satellite precipitation product (e.g. GPM IMERG) with run type/version/latency.
- Forecast rainfall.
- Modelled effective rainfall / infiltrated rainfall.
Never combine them into one unlabeled rainfall number.
11.2 Fetch and aggregation
Fetch daily/half-hourly/hourly precipitation at the field's valid source grid or available coordinate. Aggregate only the requested local-day/week/month/season windows, with timezone and accumulation semantics preserved. Convert all inputs to mm before calculation. Track whether each datum is instantaneous, interval accumulation or daily sum.
11.3 Cross-checking
A GPM IMERG cross-check can be useful, but product grids and latency differ. Do not blindly average conflicting datasets. Keep one documented primary rainfall source for each calculation, show material discrepancies and QC flags, and preserve the source snapshot used by prior reports.
11.4 Effective rainfall
Do not use a universal fixed percentage of rainfall as effective rain. Model the part entering and remaining in the root zone using supported infiltration/runoff/storage/drainage logic. In a daily root-zone depletion balance, use the actual terms (P - RO) and DP; do not subtract a separate P_effective and also subtract runoff/percolation again (that would double count losses).

If field infiltration/runoff parameters are missing, label effective rainfall as uncertain/modelled and lower confidence. If only observed rainfall is available, show the observed depth and do not pretend its effective fraction is exact.
12. Daily root-zone soil-water balance
12.1 Soil water availability
For a uniform root-zone layer:

TAW = 1000 * (theta_FC - theta_WP) * Zr
- TAW: total available root-zone water, mm.
- theta_FC: volumetric water content at field capacity, m³/m³.
- theta_WP: volumetric water content at wilting point, m³/m³.
- Zr: effective root-zone depth, m.
For layered soils, calculate layer-specific available water and the portion accessible to active roots; do not use one uniform value if the profile is materially layered.

RAW = p * TAW

p is crop- and demand-dependent depletion fraction. Use crop-specific parameters and adjust as the selected FAO method requires for ETc conditions. Do not take one generic p for every crop/stage/soil.
12.2 Daily root-zone depletion equation
FAO-style depletion balance:

Dr_i = Dr_(i-1) - (P_i - RO_i) - I_i - CR_i + ETc_adj_i + DP_i
- Dr_i: root-zone depletion at end of day i, mm.
- P_i: daily precipitation, mm.
- RO_i: runoff, mm.
- I_i: net irrigation that infiltrates the soil, mm.
- CR_i: capillary rise into root zone, mm (only when supported; otherwise explicitly assume zero).
- ETc_adj_i: water lost through crop ET under the modeled water-stress condition, mm.
- DP_i: deep percolation out of root zone, mm.
Constrain depletion to 0 <= Dr <= TAW. Water added beyond field-capacity storage is accounted as drainage/percolation, not retained indefinitely.
12.3 Initial state
Prefer measured/calibrated soil moisture at relevant root depths:

Dr_initial = 1000 * (theta_FC - theta_current) * Zr

For a layered profile, compute by layer and root fraction. If only a coarse satellite/model estimate is available, use it as a modelled initial state with depth/resolution caveat. If no moisture measure/model exists, initialize from a stated prior (e.g. last confirmed irrigation/rain event and soil-water model), propagate uncertainty and do not claim exact irrigation timing.
12.4 Water stress coefficient
While Dr <= RAW, Ks = 1.

When Dr > RAW, an FAO-56 depletion-based stress coefficient is:

Ks = (TAW - Dr) / (TAW - RAW)

Constrain Ks to [0,1]. Use the correct single/dual Kc stress adjustment described above. A modelled stress indicator is not a field diagnosis; show its assumptions and confidence.
12.5 Outputs
- Current root-zone depletion Dr (mm).
- TAW, RAW and crop p.
- Fraction of available water depleted.
- Estimated root-zone moisture, depth and source.
- Water stress coefficient Ks, if valid.
- Potential and stress-adjusted ETc.
- Daily balance table: ET₀, Kc/Kcb, Ke, ETc, observed rain, runoff estimate, irrigation event, capillary rise, drainage/percolation, start/end depletion, Ks.
- 7/14/30-day forecast scenario separately, clearly marked as forecast-driven projection, not observed fact.
13. Irrigation scheduling — when, how much, how many events and how long
13.1 The engine must not invent a fixed schedule
Do not tell every farmer to irrigate every five hours, every three days or at a fixed time. Interval, dose, pulse count and run duration depend on root-zone depletion, crop stage, soil storage/infiltration, irrigation method/capacity, system uniformity, weather forecast, water availability, and management constraints.
13.2 Irrigation trigger
- Define target allowable depletion MAD/scheduling threshold from crop sensitivity, stage, soil uncertainty and farmer/management preference.
- Use RAW = p * TAW as a biophysical no-stress threshold; management may set a more conservative allowable depletion where justified.
- Forecast depletion forward with ETc and expected rainfall as a separate projected scenario.
- Recommend an irrigation window before/when predicted depletion reaches the chosen threshold, not merely because the daily ETc is positive.
- Sensitive stages may justify a stricter threshold only when crop-specific parameters support it.
- If the actual root-zone state is not known well enough, the result is Irrigation review suggested rather than an exact automated dose.
13.3 Net irrigation depth
At a scheduling event, calculate net refill depth based on the desired target depletion:

I_net_mm <= Dr_current - Dr_target

The upper limit is constrained by root-zone storage to field capacity, infiltration capacity, and avoidance of deep percolation. Do not recommend more water than the modeled deficit can store. If leaching for salinity is considered, it requires a separate valid salinity/leaching model and water quality data.
13.4 Gross irrigation depth
If application efficiency Ea is a measured/defensible fraction:

I_gross_mm = I_net_mm / Ea

State whether Ea includes conveyance losses, field application losses or both. Do not use an unverified default as if measured.
13.5 Convert to water volume
For field area in hectares:

Volume_m3 = I_gross_mm * Area_ha * 10

Because 1 mm over 1 ha = 10 m³. Display net and gross depth, field volume, source availability and any water cap separately.
13.6 Calculate system application rate and runtime
Required hardware/operation data:
- Irrigation system: drip / sprinkler / centre pivot / surface / other.
- Field/zone area actually irrigated.
- Pump/zone flow rate in m³/hour or L/hour.
- Emitter/nozzle count, discharge rate and spacing where relevant.
- Lateral/row spacing.
- Operating pressure and pressure variation.
- Distribution/uniformity coefficient if measured.
- System efficiency and application rate.
- Maximum allowed run time, pump schedule and water availability.
If measured zone flow Q_m3h and area A_ha are known:

Application_rate_mm_h = Q_m3h / (10 * A_ha)

Runtime_h = I_gross_mm / Application_rate_mm_h

Equivalent:

Runtime_h = Required_volume_m3 / Zone_flow_m3h

For emitter-based estimates, compute total discharge from emitter flow and the number of operating emitters, accounting for row/lateral/emitter spacing and actual irrigated area. Do not infer runtime from a farmer's two hours routine unless flow is known; duration alone does not determine volume.
13.7 How many irrigations per day / pulse scheduling
Choose number and duration of events from:
1. Target net depth and gross depth.
2. System flow and application rate.
3. Soil intake/infiltration rate and surface storage.
4. Root-zone storage and wetting pattern.
5. Runoff/ponding risk.
6. Crop rooting and sensitivity.
7. Emitter wetting bulb and system uniformity for drip.
8. Wind/evaporation constraints for sprinkler systems.
9. Power/water supply and operational limits.
If the system's application rate exceeds the soil's intake/storage capacity, or field slope/soil crusting creates runoff risk, the schedule may split into cycles with soak intervals. Calculate cycle count and duration from a validated field infiltration/system model or measured irrigation audit. Do not prescribe every 5 hours without those inputs. For drip, small frequent irrigation may be appropriate in some coarse soils and crops, but exact frequency requires soil/root-zone and emitter data.
13.8 Irrigation timing of day
Choose an operational window based on water availability, energy constraints, system rules, weather and crop-management practice. Avoid universal claims that one clock time is always scientifically optimal. For sprinkler irrigation, wind drift/evaporation conditions can matter; for drip, evaporative timing is less directly important but soil/water scheduling still governs. Use farm timezone and forecast wind/heat as scheduling context.
13.9 Irrigation result shown to farmer
The recommendation drawer must state:
- Field and crop/stage.
- Why now — current depletion, RAW/MAD threshold and expected depletion trajectory.
- Recommended net depth and gross depth.
- Estimated total volume (m³) and area basis.
- Method and efficiency assumption.
- Zone flow/application rate.
- Estimated run duration and number of cycles only if hardware/infiltration data are known.
- Rainfall forecast considered (separately, with probability and time window).
- Water supply/cap constraints.
- Expected post-irrigation depletion/storage.
- Risks of over-irrigation/leaching/runoff.
- Confidence, missing inputs and source/method version.
- Record as planned versus Record irrigation completed (distinct actions).
Never automatically record a recommendation as actual irrigation.
PART D — SOIL, NUTRIENTS AND FERTILISATION
14. Soil & Land page
14.1 Properties to display when genuinely available
- Sand, silt, clay fractions and textural class.
- pH.
- Organic carbon and organic matter (stored separately with conversion method if one is derived).
- Bulk density and coarse fragments.
- Cation exchange capacity (CEC).
- Total/mineral nitrogen where source supports it.
- Available phosphorus and potassium with extraction method.
- Secondary/micronutrient test results when available.
- Electrical conductivity (EC)/salinity and test method.
- Field capacity, wilting point and available water capacity where supported.
- Drainage classification, waterlogging history.
- Slope/elevation/terrain.
- Soil moisture by depth/time/product.
- Laboratory sample ID, date, depth, lab/method and farmer/source.
14.2 Structured measurement table
Columns: property, value, unit, depth, evidence type, source/product/lab, sample/observation date, retrieval date, spatial resolution, uncertainty/range, confidence, What it means, status. Keep lab sample separate from gridded predictions. If mapped estimate and newer laboratory result conflict, show both and apply a documented precedence rule; never overwrite the laboratory record.
14.3 Soil texture and derived parameters
- Texture fractions should sum to approximately 100% within a documented rounding tolerance.
- Classify with a declared system (e.g. USDA texture triangle); do not mix systems silently.
- Do not derive actual salinity, measured nutrient deficiency, drainage failure or exact available water capacity from unrelated soil properties.
- Pedotransfer estimates of field capacity/wilting point may only be used if a named, validated function and all required inputs exist; label estimated and carry uncertainty.
14.4 Soil data provider status
Potential source: SoilGrids mapped soil predictions at 250 m and standard depth intervals, with uncertainty. These are regional gridded predictions, not lab data from the selected field. As of 15 November 2026, the official ISRIC SoilGrids page reports REST API service issues and a temporary pause; do not build a live green connected status around that REST endpoint until service availability is rechecked. Use farmer lab data, verified local/national soil providers or an approved cached/download workflow as available. If no reliable source exists, show Not available and offer Add measurement.
14.5 Terrain
Use a verified elevation/DEM source and record resolution, projection and vertical datum. Slope is derived from elevation with a documented algorithm and correct horizontal/vertical units. Terrain can inform runoff/erosion susceptibility; it alone does not prove waterlogging, soil loss or drainage failure.
15. Nutrient intelligence and fertilizer calculations
15.1 No generic fertilizer-dose API
Weather APIs do not prescribe fertilizer rates. Crop databases may provide nutrient demand/uptake curves, but actual field recommendations require soil tests (with extraction method/depth), crop/cultivar and stage, attainable target yield, prior crops/residues, manure/compost, irrigation-water nutrients, fertilizer history, local calibration and fertilizer-product composition. A global crop name plus weather is not enough to generate a safe N/P/K prescription.
15.2 Inputs needed per field
- Crop and cultivar; crop stage; planting date.
- Crop nutrient uptake curve or nutrient requirement per unit of attainable yield, with source and uncertainty.
- Target yield and its basis/source; actual historical yields if confirmed.
- Soil-test N/P/K, sample date/depth, extraction method, lab and units.
- Organic matter/mineralization context only where the selected model supports it.
- Previous crop, residues, cover crops and validated legume N credit if relevant.
- Manure/compost type, nutrient analysis, rate, date and expected availability.
- Irrigation-water nutrient analysis where relevant.
- Fertilizer product grade/composition, nutrient form, application method, timing and prior amount.
- Local calibrated recommendations, regulatory limits, environmental restrictions and application rules.
- Expected nutrient-use/recovery efficiency and its source/method.
15.3 Nutrient budget logic
Calculate each nutrient separately (N, P, K and any secondary/micronutrients). Use a locally calibrated agronomic method. A general bookkeeping framework is:

Nutrient_gap = crop_nutrient_requirement - soil_supply - available_residue_credit - available_manure_credit - water_supply_credit - other_supported_credits

Then, where a validated recovery-efficiency calculation is available:

Fertilizer_nutrient_rate = max(0, Nutrient_gap) / expected_fertilizer_recovery_fraction

This is a framework, not a universal prescription formula. Soil-test calibration curves, nutrient availability transformations, fixation/mineralization and response functions must be specific to crop, soil, region and nutrient. Do not double-count total soil nutrient stock as immediately plant-available supply.

For N, estimate soil supply and crop demand with locally validated methods; omission-plot or calibrated regional recommendations may be necessary. For P and K, incorporate soil-test calibration and crop removal/uptake and residual nutrient credits. Crop uptake per tonne of yield is not automatically equal to fertilizer rate.
15.4 Fertilizer material amount
If a validated nutrient application target is known and product label grade gives nutrient fraction:

Product_kg_per_ha = required_nutrient_kg_per_ha / nutrient_mass_fraction

Total_product_kg = Product_kg_per_ha * field_area_ha

For example, 46% N product has a nitrogen mass fraction of 0.46. The application schedule must split the validated seasonal requirement according to crop uptake/stage, local agronomic guidance, nutrient mobility, soil texture, irrigation method and loss risk. The UI must show elemental nutrient basis versus oxide basis (e.g. P vs P2O5, K vs K2O) explicitly and convert only with documented factors.
15.5 Timing / split applications
- Load crop-stage nutrient uptake and critical demand windows from the crop knowledge base.
- Compare current crop stage and actual date to those windows.
- Consider nutrient mobility, leaching/volatilization/runoff risk, soil moisture and weather forecast.
- Split nutrients when crop-specific/local guidance supports it; do not universally split all products the same way.
- Use prior applications, residues and manure credits to prevent duplicate recommendation.
- Fertigation requires a compatible irrigation system, product solubility/compatibility and controlled injector/calibration. Warn about contamination risk and distribution uniformity.
- Foliar applications require crop/product-specific safety, concentration and compatibility support; do not infer a safe spray concentration from nutrient deficit.
15.6 Fertilizer record and UI
Show plan vs actual separately:
- Nutrient / product / grade.
- Calculated nutrient requirement and product amount.
- Rate per hectare and total field quantity.
- Planned split events by date/stage.
- Method (broadcast, band/placement, fertigation, foliar) and method constraints.
- Source, lab test, yield target, crop uptake model and efficiency assumption.
- Farmer-confirmed application event, date, quantity and evidence.
- Nutrient balance and historical applications.
- Environmental cautions and local regulatory warnings.
Do not generate a fertilizer recommendation if soil/crop inputs are inadequate. Show Need a current soil test or Needs local agronomic calibration instead. Do not give an exact nutrient rate based only on satellite greenness.
PART E — VEGETATION, CROP PROTECTION AND RISK
16. Satellite and vegetation intelligence
16.1 Sources and acquisition
Potential products include Sentinel-2 surface reflectance, MODIS vegetation indices and Harmonized Landsat/Sentinel products, but only after the precise product, access method, licence, band definition, quality mask, pixel size and composite period are implemented. Sentinel-2 MSI has 13 bands; visible/NIR bands include 10 m products, while red-edge/SWIR bands have 20 m resolution and atmospheric bands 60 m. This scale must be displayed; a field smaller than a pixel cannot produce fine field diagnostics.
16.2 NDVI
NDVI = (NIR - Red) / (NIR + Red)

For Sentinel-2, the red and NIR reflectance bands (typically B4 and B8) are used with the relevant reflectance scaling and quality masking. Apply cloud, cloud-shadow, cirrus and no-data masking using product QA/classification. Check reflectance scaling and divide-by-zero/missing conditions.
16.3 Field extraction
For each image/composite:
1. Load valid surface reflectance product.
2. Apply QA/cloud/shadow masks.
3. Intersect valid pixels with the selected field geometry version.
4. Calculate polygon-weighted mean/median and distribution only over valid pixels.
5. Count valid/invalid pixels and report valid coverage percentage.
6. Store acquisition date, composite window, pixel size, product version, mask, algorithm and geometry version.
7. Compare only compatible products, crop stages and seasonal baselines.
Display NDVI/EVI name, value/scale, date, source, pixel size, valid-pixel count, coverage, cloud quality, aggregate method, trend/baseline and uncertainty. NDVI signals spectral vegetation change; it does not prove disease, nitrogen deficiency or yield loss.
16.4 Time series and change detection
Delta_NDVI = current_valid_NDVI - previous_comparable_NDVI

Display actual time-series samples, not interpolated fake data. Require enough valid observations and coverage. Stage-aware baselines require crop identity/stage and compatible reference data. If source/product/season/geometry differs, label Not directly comparable.
17. Crop-protection risks and environmental risk
17.1 Potential risk domains
- Water stress / depletion.
- Heat exposure during sensitive stage.
- Cold/frost exposure where relevant.
- Dry spell / rainfall deficit.
- Salinity risk (requires soil/water EC and crop tolerance).
- Potential runoff/erosion susceptibility.
- Nutrient deficiency/excess only from valid test/model evidence.
- Crop growth delay or stage deviation.
- Vegetation decline (not a diagnosis).
- Pest/disease risk only where crop-pathogen/host relationships and a validated risk model/scouting evidence exist.
- Irrigation system malfunction only from actual operation/sensor/user evidence.
- Rotation constraints and crop-family repetition.
17.2 Risk record
Each risk stores: rule/model ID and version; field/crop occurrence; severity; confidence; onset/last-seen; evidence IDs; source timestamps; thresholds; calculation; plausible mechanism; competing explanations; mitigation/monitoring action; and status history.

Severity and confidence are independent. High severity / low confidence means the possible consequence is serious but evidence is weak; it does not become a confirmed diagnosis.
17.3 Example rules
- Water stress: valid root-zone balance shows depletion reaching/exceeding crop-specific RAW/MAD threshold.
- Heat exposure: validated crop-stage-specific temperature-duration threshold exceeded.
- Rainfall deficit: comparable season-period rainfall below its local historical baseline by documented threshold.
- Vegetation change: quality-screened comparable time series has statistically/operationally meaningful reduction.
- Soil-data gap: required soil variable absent for a requested model.
- Rotation review: review date reached or material profile/crop history change invalidates suitability.
17.4 Pest and disease warning
Weather factors (temperature, humidity, leaf wetness, rainfall, wind) may contribute to a pathogen risk model only when species/variety, pathogen, local epidemiological parameters, phenological stage and model validation are known. A humidity spike or NDVI decline alone cannot diagnose a disease. A model alert says Risk conditions favourable for ... and gives its source/rule; confirmation requires scouting/lab/qualified agronomic diagnosis as applicable. No generic pesticide dose or spray interval may be generated from the climate API alone.
17.5 Alert lifecycle
New → Active → Acknowledged → Resolved; optional Snoozed/Dismissed states have separate semantics. Dismissed is not corrected. Deduplicate the same unresolved cause; retain first/last seen and all status changes. A task completion does not auto-resolve an alert; later evidence must verify it.
18. Field actions, observations and operational records
18.1 Records supported per field
- Irrigation events.
- Fertilizer/nutrient events.
- Planting/transplant/sowing events.
- Harvest events and actual yield/quality.
- Tillage, residue, cover crop and amendment events.
- Scouting observations and photos.
- Pest/disease observations.
- Soil sampling/laboratory results.
- Machinery/irrigation-system maintenance.
- Tasks and recommendations.
Each record includes field, date/time/timezone, event type, quantity/unit/area where relevant, author, source type, original user statement, attachments, confirmation state and edit history.
18.2 Routine vs event
A saved recurring routine describes what usually happens. An event describes what actually happened on a particular date. Never generate actual irrigation/fertilizer/planting events from a routine. If the routine is old, show Needs confirmation and disclose the affected calculation.
18.3 Tasks
Task states: Suggested, Accepted, Scheduled, In progress, Completed, Deferred, Dismissed. Task payload includes title, reason, field/crop stage, due window, priority, source evidence, prerequisites, notes, actor, completion timestamp and any linked alert/report. The due date is an estimate if based on a model; label it accordingly.
19. Crop yield, harvest and productivity
- Store actual harvested quantity, harvest area, unit, moisture basis/standardization when relevant, crop/variety, field, harvest date, grade/quality, source and farmer confirmation.
- Compute yield only as harvested quantity / harvested area after checking compatible area and unit basis.
- Store multiple harvests/cuttings separately for forage/perennial crops.
- Yield projections require an explicitly named validated model with crop, cultivar, weather, soil, water, planting/stage, management and historical calibration inputs. Do not infer a precise yield solely from NDVI or a generic health score.
- Compare yield by compatible crop, unit, season, moisture basis, cultivar and area basis. Distinguish observed yield from modelled forecast.
